ious play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**Decision Time in Durak: The Rocket or the Crouch?**\n\nAlright, here\'s the situation. I\'m the Landlord, playing solo against two peasants. My hand is decent: a Triple 7s, a single 8, 10, K, and my prized Rocket (Black and Red Jokers).  The Right-side peasant just dropped a brutal Full House of Triple 2s and a pair of 3s – the absolute best.  My turn.\n\nMy instinct screams "Rocket!" - it\'s the only way to beat that Full House. But... is it smart?\n\nIf I play the Rocket, I\'m left with a 777-8, and two singles. I lead with the triplet and the other peasants have a ton of cards still. They likely have strong hands too and could easily top my low triplet. This leaves me vulnerable with only the weaker singles left. If that 777-8 gets topped, my chance of re-entry is over.\n\nIf I pass, I keep the Rocket. My thought process is if I can conserve the Rocket and split the Jokers, I can ensure at least two entries later in the game. I think by passing, I save the potential of using it later to guarantee a win or stopping a winning run. Breaking the Rocket creates two insane singles (Red, Black Joker) that the opponents probably can\'t top. This would be a smart move, giving me ultimate control over singles.\n\nThe Right-side peasant\'s play screams "clear my hand!". He has 12 cards left. If I pass, I take the risk he clears his hand. Left has 17 cards, and is also dangerous. By playing the Rocket, I leave myself too vulnerable.\n\nSo, I decide to crouch. Pass. It\'s the only viable option. I need to keep the Rocket for a better time or the option to split it into two winning singles.  I wait for the perfect moment.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The opponent (Right) has played the highest possible Full House (Rank 2). While I can beat this with a Rocket, doing so would leave me with a mediocre hand consisting of a mid-ranked triplet (777), and loose singles (8, 10, K). With the Left opponent holding 17 cards, it is highly probable they possess a triplet higher than 7s (such as 9s, Js, Qs, or As) or a bomb. If I Rocket and lead my triplet, and it gets beaten, I will be left with small singles and no way to regain control, leading to a loss.\n\nBy passing, I retain the Rocket. I can choose to use it later as a bomb or, more likely, split the Jokers into two unbeatable singles (since three 2s are already played) to control the game when opponents play single cards. This flexibility offers a much higher chance of winning than an all-in play now.\n\nReason: My remaining hand after using the Rocket (777-8, 10, K) is not strong enough to clear the table against an opponent with 17 cards who likely holds higher triplets. Preserving the Jokers as top singles or a defensive Rocket is the superior strategy.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
